---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';
import { workingPapers, featured } from '../../data/research';
import { bibtex, plainCitation, scholarlyArticle } from '../../lib/cite';

const p = workingPapers[0];
const url = new URL(p.page!, site.url).href;

// Google Scholar reads these tags to index a paper page.
const meta = [
  { name: 'citation_title', content: p.title },
  { name: 'citation_author', content: 'Maskey, Avinash' },
  { name: 'citation_author_institution', content: 'Kathmandu University' },
  { name: 'citation_author_email', content: site.emailPrimary },
  { name: 'citation_publication_date', content: '2026/10' },
  { name: 'citation_technical_report_institution', content: 'Kathmandu University' },
  { name: 'citation_abstract_html_url', content: url },
  { name: 'citation_language', content: 'en' },
  { name: 'citation_keywords', content: featured.keywords.join('; ') },
  ...(p.pdf ? [{ name: 'citation_pdf_url', content: new URL(p.pdf, site.url).href }] : []),
];

const article = {
  ...scholarlyArticle(p),
  abstract: featured.abstract,
  keywords: featured.keywords.join(', '),
  about: ['Nepal Stock Exchange', 'financial connectedness', 'forecasting', 'data quality'],
  isBasedOn: { '@type': 'WebPage', name: 'NEPSE index history (ShareSansar)', url: 'https://www.sharesansar.com/indices-sub-indices' },
};

const toc = [
  { id: 'abstract', label: 'Abstract' },
  { id: 'questions', label: 'Research questions' },
  { id: 'data', label: 'Data' },
  { id: 'methods', label: 'Methods' },
  { id: 'forecasting', label: 'Forecasting and trading test' },
  { id: 'results', label: 'Main results' },
  { id: 'reproducibility', label: 'Reproducibility' },
  { id: 'cite', label: 'How to cite' },
];
const bibId = 'bib-feature';
const mailto = `mailto:${site.emailPrimary}?subject=${encodeURIComponent('Manuscript request: ' + p.title)}`;
---
<Base
  title="NEPSE Sector Connectedness and Lead-Lag"
  description="Working paper by Avinash Maskey on shock spillovers across ten Nepal Stock Exchange sector indices, using Granger tests, Diebold-Yilmaz connectedness and ML."
  ogType="article"
  meta={meta}
  jsonld={[article]}
  toc={toc}
>
  <header class="page-title">
    <span class="m">{p.status}<br />Oct {p.year}</span>
    <h1 class="long">{p.title}</h1>
    <p class="authorline">
      <strong>{site.name}</strong><br />
      <span>{site.department}, {site.school}, {site.institution}, Nepal</span>
    </p>
    <p class="tags" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
      {p.pdf ? <a class="tag strong" href={p.pdf}>PDF</a> : <a class="tag strong" href={mailto}>Request the manuscript</a>}
      <a class="tag" href="#cite">BibTeX</a>
      <a class="tag" href="/research/">All research</a>
    </p>
  </header>

  <Section id="abstract" num="1" title="Abstract">
    <p>{featured.abstract}</p>
    <p class="keywords"><i>Keywords:</i> {featured.keywords.join(', ')}.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="questions" num="2" title="Research questions">
    <ol class="questions">
      {featured.questions.map((q) => <li>{q}</li>)}
    </ol>
    <p>
      The paper makes four contributions. It documents a date-alignment protocol for compiled
      market data and shows on real and simulated data that a one-day misalignment manufactures
      Granger causality, a data-quality point that applies to any study joining series from
      different sources. It gives a broad picture of connectedness across ten to twelve NEPSE
      sectors and shows how the roles of banks and hydropower changed. It explains a puzzle in
      market-lead regressions. And it shows that tree-based models find a real but small
      directional signal that does not survive realistic trading costs, as weak-form efficiency
      with costs would predict.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="data" num="3" title="Data">
    <h3 class="inline" style="margin-top: 0;">Source and coverage</h3>
    <p>
      Daily closing values and turnover for 18 NEPSE indices were collected from the public index
      history published by <a href="https://www.sharesansar.com/indices-sub-indices">ShareSansar</a>,
      using a purpose-built scraper that saves one file per index. The NEPSE index runs from
      3 January 2010 and most sector sub-indices from 20 March 2011, to 16 October 2026. The raw
      files are frozen with SHA-256 checksums, so any later change to them stops the pipeline.
    </p>

    <h3 class="inline">Audit and alignment</h3>
    <p>
      Every index was audited against the NEPSE trading calendar of 3,844 trading days for
      missing and extra days, duplicate dates, non-positive values, large jumps, runs of identical
      closing values and shifted dates. A rolling correlation scan compared each sector’s same-day
      correlation with NEPSE against its correlation at shifts of one and two days. In the new
      data, the same-day correlation is highest for every operating sector except the trading
      index during 2017.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same audit applied to the compiled file used in my master’s thesis produced different
      results. Its finance column held the next trading day’s official value on 1,408 dates between
      2014 and mid 2020; its hydropower column was two days ahead in 2016 to 2018; and its hotels
      column was one or two days behind in 2015 to 2020. The errors came from how the file was
      compiled, not from the source. Because NEPSE values are rounded until mid 2015 and the
      reported percentage change is unreliable before 2016, all returns are recomputed from
      closing values. Panels are built under six rules:
    </p>
    <ol class="rules">
      <li>The NEPSE index defines the trading calendar.</li>
      <li>Each index keeps only its own published values; nothing is forward-filled.</li>
      <li>A day enters a panel only if every member has a value.</li>
      <li>Returns are computed after matching, so each return covers the same two days for every sector.</li>
      <li>Returns spanning a closure longer than 21 calendar days are removed (the 2015 earthquake and two COVID-19 closures in 2020).</li>
      <li>Runs of five or more identical closing values are treated as publication failures and blanked.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3 class="inline">Trading-week changes</h3>
    <p>
      NEPSE changed its trading week four times in the sample: Sunday to Thursday until
      16 May 2022, Monday to Friday until mid June 2022, a six-day week from 19 June to
      16 September 2022, Sunday to Thursday again until 5 April 2026, and Monday to Friday since.
      Each regime is recorded in the configuration file and checked automatically against the
      days on which NEPSE actually traded. Each return carries a flag for the first session after
      a weekend or holiday.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="booktabs">
        <caption><b>Table 1.</b> Analysis panels. Days are daily returns after removing returns that span long closures. The NEPSE index enters every panel as a market control, never as a sector.</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Panel</th><th scope="col">Period</th><th scope="col" class="num">Sectors</th><th scope="col" class="num">Days</th><th scope="col">Members</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {featured.panels.map((r) => (
            <tr><td class="nowrap"><strong>{r.panel}</strong></td><td class="nowrap">{r.period}</td><td class="num">{r.sectors}</td><td class="num">{r.days}</td><td class="small">{r.members}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="methods" num="4" title="Methods">
    <p>
      Returns are daily log returns in percent, computed from closing values. Unit roots and
      stationarity are checked with augmented Dickey-Fuller and KPSS tests, and normality and
      autocorrelation with Jarque-Bera and Ljung-Box tests.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sector returns are modelled jointly as a vector autoregression with the lag order chosen
      by the Akaike criterion (Schwarz and Hannan-Quinn as checks). Granger causality is tested
      equation by equation with Wald statistics that use HC1 heteroskedasticity-consistent
      standard errors, because returns show strong volatility clustering. With ten sectors there
      are 90 directed pairs, so p-values are corrected with the Holm and Benjamini-Hochberg
      procedures.
    </p>
    <p>
      Generalized impulse responses, which do not depend on the ordering of the variables, show
      how a shock to one sector reaches the others. The Diebold-Yilmaz framework turns the
      generalized forecast error variance decomposition at a ten-day horizon into total,
      directional and net spillover measures. Connectedness over time is traced with rolling
      windows and with a time-varying parameter VAR. Finally, market-lead regressions are
      rewritten to separate relative momentum from the lead of the market index.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="forecasting" num="5" title="Forecasting and trading test">
    <p>
      The last 500 trading days of each panel, from 7 August 2024 to 16 October 2026, form the
      test period. Every forecast of day <i>t</i> uses only data up to day <i>t</i>−1, and
      hyperparameters were fixed in advance rather than tuned on the test period. Six models
      forecast each sector’s next-day return: a random walk, the historical mean, a univariate AR
      model, a rolling VAR, a random forest and XGBoost. The two tree models use 66 inputs: five
      lags of every sector return and of the NEPSE return, and the previous day’s change in log
      turnover for each series.
    </p>
    <p>
      Accuracy is measured as each model’s RMSE relative to the random walk and tested with the
      Diebold-Mariano statistic with the Harvey-Leybourne-Newbold correction. SHAP values show
      which inputs the tree models use. A long-or-cash rule then turns each forecast into a
      position, since short selling is not allowed on NEPSE. The baseline cost is 0.345% per buy
      or sell (a 0.33% broker commission plus the 0.015% SEBON fee), with 0.255% for the largest
      trades, and the break-even cost is reported for each strategy.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="results" num="6" title="Main results">
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="booktabs">
        <caption><b>Table 2.</b> Headline results, main panel</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Result</th><th scope="col" class="num">Value</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {featured.findings.map((f) => <tr><td>{f.label}</td><td class="num">{f.value}</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      About 78% of a sector’s ten-day forecast uncertainty comes from other sectors. Commercial
      banks changed from the main transmitter into a net receiver, while hydropower became a
      transmitter. The results hold across lag orders, window lengths, a TVP-VAR, forecast
      horizons and the changes in NEPSE’s trading week. For investors, diversification across
      NEPSE sectors protects less than it used to. For researchers, the study shows what tested,
      reproducible data pipelines add to empirical finance.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="reproducibility" num="7" title="Reproducibility">
    <p>
      Every step is a numbered Python script driven by one configuration file, using statsmodels,
      pandas, NumPy, scikit-learn, XGBoost and SHAP. Fifteen automatic tests check the data
      contracts and the methods. One test plants a known lead in simulated data and checks that
      it is found; another shifts one series by a day and checks that a false Granger link
      appears. A full run from the frozen raw files reproduces every reported number with a
      single command.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <p class="label">Data and code availability</p>
      <p>
        The raw index files, the cleaned panels, the full pipeline, the tests and the scripts
        behind every table and figure are available from the author.
        Please <a href={mailto}>contact me</a> for the manuscript or the replication files.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="cite" num="8" title="How to cite">
    <p class="cite-box">{plainCitation(p)}</p>
    <div class="bib">
      <pre id={bibId}>{bibtex(p)}</pre>
      <button type="button" class="copy" data-copy={bibId}>Copy</button>
    </div>
  </Section>
</Base>
